Title: The Tell-Tale Heart

Credit: Written by  
Author: Edgar Allan Poe  
Source: Adapted from book  
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A dimly lit room, shadows dance across the walls. THE NARRATOR stands in the doorway, eyes wide with a mix of fear and excitement.

NARRATOR  
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? 

The NARRATOR steps into the room, moving cautiously.

NARRATOR  
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. 

He pauses, listening intently.

NARRATOR  
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? 

He approaches the OLD MAN, who lies asleep in bed.

NARRATOR  
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. 

The OLD MAN stirs slightly, his pale blue eye visible through the darkness.

NARRATOR  
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. But his eye! Yes, it was this! 

The NARRATOR's expression turns to one of rage.

NARRATOR  
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. 

He takes a deep breath, calming himself.

NARRATOR  
So by degrees, I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT - LATER

The NARRATOR stands at the door, a dark lantern in hand. He opens the door slowly, peering inside.

NARRATOR  
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! 

He pushes the door open, inch by inch, revealing the OLD MAN asleep.

NARRATOR  
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed.

He holds the lantern, a thin ray of light illuminating the OLD MAN's eye.

NARRATOR  
And then, when my head was well in the room, I undid the lantern cautiously—oh, so cautiously. 

The OLD MAN suddenly stirs, sitting up in bed.

OLD MAN  
Who’s there?

The NARRATOR freezes, heart racing.

NARRATOR  
I kept quite still and said nothing. 

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT - MOMENTS LATER

The OLD MAN listens intently, fear evident on his face.

NARRATOR  
I heard a slight groan, the groan of mortal terror. 

The OLD MAN's breathing quickens, and the NARRATOR watches with a twisted smile.

NARRATOR  
Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him.

The NARRATOR opens the lantern just a crack, a dim ray falling on the OLD MAN's eye.

NARRATOR  
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. 

He grips the lantern tighter, the heartbeat of the OLD MAN echoing in his ears.

NARRATOR  
I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart. 

The heartbeat grows louder, matching the NARRATOR's rising fury.

NARRATOR  
But even yet I refrained and kept still. 

Suddenly, the heartbeat becomes deafening.

NARRATOR  
The old man’s hour had come! 

With a loud yell, he throws open the lantern and leaps into the room.

OLD MAN  
Ahhh!

The OLD MAN shrieks once, and the NARRATOR drags him to the floor.

NARRATOR  
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. 

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT - LATER

The NARRATOR stands over the corpse, breathing heavily.

NARRATOR  
I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs. 

He works quickly, placing the pieces beneath the floorboards.

NARRATOR  
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye could have detected anything wrong. 

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT - MOMENTS LATER

The NARRATOR hears a KNOCK at the door. He straightens up, a smile on his face.

NARRATOR  
There entered three men, officers of the police. 

The officers enter, looking around the room.

OFFICER REYNOLDS  
A shriek had been heard by a neighbor during the night. 

The NARRATOR leads them through the house, feigning innocence.

NARRATOR  
The old man was absent in the country. 

They sit, and the NARRATOR places his chair over the spot where the body lies.

NARRATOR  
I was singularly at ease. 

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT - MOMENTS LATER

The officers chat, but the NARRATOR grows increasingly anxious.

NARRATOR  
I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. 

The heartbeat begins to echo in his mind, growing louder.

NARRATOR  
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. 

He gasps for breath, trying to maintain his composure.

NARRATOR  
I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling: but it continued and gained definiteness. 

The heartbeat grows louder, drowning out the officers' voices.

NARRATOR  
Oh God! What could I do? 

He stands, pacing the floor, his anxiety boiling over.

NARRATOR  
I foamed—I raved—I swore! 

Suddenly, he can take it no longer.

NARRATOR  
Villains! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

FADE OUT.
